{"tool_name":"slam-dunk","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"SLAM-DUNK","home":"http://t-neumann.github.io/slamdunk","license":"AGPL-3.0","summary":"Fully automated software tool for automated, robust, scalable and reproducible SLAMseq data analysis.","addition_date":"2019-08-09T13:19:26Z","last_update_date":"2020-06-16T10:55:26Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:SLAM-DUNK","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"tobias.neumann@imp.ac.at","name":"Tobias Neumann","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3908-4224","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"philipp.rescheneder@gmail.com","name":"Philipp Rescheneder","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://t-neumann.github.io/slamdunk/docs.html#slam-dunk"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2495"},{"term":"Quantification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3799"},{"term":"SNP detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0484"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"SLAM-DUNK","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"hans","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/S12859-019-2849-7","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2019 The Author(s).Background: Methods to read out naturally occurring or experimentally introduced nucleic acid modifications are emerging as powerful tools to study dynamic cellular processes. The recovery, quantification and interpretation of such events in high-throughput sequencing datasets demands specialized bioinformatics approaches. Results: Here, we present Digital Unmasking of Nucleotide conversions in K-mers (DUNK), a data analysis pipeline enabling the quantification of nucleotide conversions in high-throughput sequencing datasets. We demonstrate using experimentally generated and simulated datasets that DUNK allows constant mapping rates irrespective of nucleotide-conversion rates, promotes the recovery of multimapping reads and employs Single Nucleotide Polymorphism (SNP) masking to uncouple true SNPs from nucleotide conversions to facilitate a robust and sensitive quantification of nucleotide-conversions. As a first application, we implement this strategy as SLAM-DUNK for the analysis of SLAMseq profiles, in which 4-thiouridine-labeled transcripts are detected based on T > C conversions. SLAM-DUNK provides both raw counts of nucleotide-conversion containing reads as well as a base-content and read coverage normalized approach for estimating the fractions of labeled transcripts as readout. Conclusion: Beyond providing a readily accessible tool for analyzing SLAMseq and related time-resolved RNA sequencing methods (TimeLapse-seq, TUC-seq), DUNK establishes a broadly applicable strategy for quantifying nucleotide conversions.","authors":[{"name":"Ameres S.L."},{"name":"Herzog V.A."},{"name":"Muhar M."},{"name":"Neumann T."},{"name":"Rescheneder P."},{"name":"Von Haeseler A."},{"name":"Zuber J."}],"citationCount":20,"date":"2019-05-20T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"Quantification of experimentally induced nucleotide conversions in high-throughput sequencing datasets"},"pmcid":"PMC6528199","pmid":"31109287","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"DNA polymorphism","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2885"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"},{"term":"Transcription factors and regulatory sites","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0749"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"SLAM-DUNK","home":"https://bio.tools/SLAM-DUNK","license":"AGPL-3.0","summary":"Fully automated software tool for automated, robust, scalable and reproducible SLAMseq data analysis.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:slam-dunk/cmd/t-neumann.github.io","@license":"https:/